### RIFT KV Address Block Extension
Sandy Zhang presented [01-RIFT-KV-addressblock](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-rift-01-rift-kv-addressblock-00).
* **Overview:** Proposed a mechanism using RIFT Key-Value (KV) extensions to distribute dynamic IP address blocks from top-of-fabric (northernmost) switches to leaf switches in AI/Data Center (AIDC) environments. Leaves act as DHCP servers to allocate addresses to connected GPUs, allowing address aggregation and minimizing routing table size.
* **Discussion:**
  * **Targeting and Collision:** Jeff Tantsura noted that KV messages are flooded down to all leaves. It is unclear how a leaf identifies which IP block is intended for it to prevent overlapping address allocations between leaves.
  * **Persistence vs. Ephemeral State:** Jeff Tantsura and Christian Hopps raised concerns that routing protocol state is ephemeral. Dynamic allocations may lead to renumbering or address block churn upon switch reboots or topology changes, unlike static DHCP server pool configurations.
  * **Comparison to Existing Protocols:** Jeff Tantsura suggested comparing this approach against established dynamic allocation mechanisms such as SLAAC.

